<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bushido</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bushido, meaning &quot;the way of the warrior&quot;, is the code of conduct and ethics associated with the Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a>, stressing loyalty, honour, discipline and martial skill. It was codified largely during the peaceful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a> it reflected the increasingly ritual and aesthetic rather than practical character of samurai military culture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bushido.jpg" alt="Bushido" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Felice Beato · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Koboto_Santaro,_a_Japanese_military_commander_Wellcome_V0037661.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Bushido is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> moral code concerning attitudes, behaviour and lifestyle. Its origins date to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> period (1185 to 1333), but the code was formalised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> (1603 to 1868). The word is rarely attested in pre-modern literature and first appears in the Kōyō Gunkan of about 1616, a military chronicle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-clan">Takeda clan</a>. The ideas it formalised appeared as unwritten customs in the 12th century under the shogun Minamoto Yoritomo, and a Way of the Bow and the Horse existed from that century. During the Muromachi period (1336 to 1573) the way of the warrior was refined by adding Zen meditation, painting, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japanese-tea-ceremony">tea ceremony</a> and poetry, including the death poem written before battle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Bushido was never a single code that every <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> had to follow. Interpretations varied by clan, by individual and by era. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku</a> bushido put honour, warfare, weapon mastery and valour first. Samurai were as practical on the battlefield as other warriors, sometimes retreated from unwinnable battles, and did not seek an honourable death, which was honourable only in the service of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> and while furthering his cause.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> then enjoyed two and a half centuries of relative peace under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a>, and bushido was refined from a focus on valour in battle to one on moral integrity. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miyamoto-musashi">Miyamoto Musashi</a> (1584 to 1645) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamamoto-tsunetomo">Yamamoto Tsunetomo</a> (1659 to 1719) wrote at length about their interpretations. Influences included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zen">Zen Buddhism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Meiji restoration abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> class in the 1870s. Bushido of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-era">Meiji era</a> stressed loyalty to the emperor and self-sacrifice more than many Tokugawa interpretations did. Nitobe Inazō's book Bushido: The Soul of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> made it known abroad, though scholars regard the morals he described as romanticised. In Shōwa Japan before and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> bushido was used for militarism, to present war as purifying and death as a duty. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taisho-period">Taishō period</a> it was advocated as the way of the merchant, and it is still invoked in business and martial arts.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bushido” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Schools of swordsmanship (kenjutsu) multiplied, <b>bushido</b> ethics were set out in works such as the Hagakure, and the martial arts were refined in the Edo period; Tokugawa military life became more cultural than functional.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Military</a></li></ul></section>
